  "notes": "Boston-to-New York micro-chop at volume. The documented parameter is the workflow migration — he left the MPC for Pro Tools only — so this is the tightest quantize on the dusty half of the axis (0.9) with bright, clap-layered modern drums sitting over a dusty loop. The second parameter is the micro-chopping: harmony is reassembled from many short slices, which is why `melody.densityPerBar` is high, `motifPitchCount` is wide and `intervals.leap` is nearly triple the genre's — a re-sequenced slice pile does not move stepwise. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it medium-high: the workflow and method are first-person, but the BPM band and every drum probability here are catalog-level inference from \"kick 1 and 3.5, hard snare 2&4, 8th hats with light swing\". The specific inferred parameter is `drums.snare.layerClapOffsetMs` — the research says \"layered claps\" and nothing about how far apart. ⚠ The third progression family (i–VI–ii–v) is INFERRED; the research names only i–iv and ii–V–i, and it carries the lowest weight so the two researched families dominate. ⚠ `snareRoll.pitchWalk` is inherited rather than authored — the research says nothing about how he pitches a roll.",
